 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0305a43d-bf81-5ecb-8a77-49a30ade0147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345030 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:  \n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as it has an entry not equal to $1$  on the main diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal. \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98334c69-2d0e-5223-82ea-d968a6c359cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395818 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{5}{2},{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{5}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395819 #edb/ref 1077 